WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.020
Parcels for the mail train are strapped in

00:00:03.020 --> 00:00:04.030
bundles of four.

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:07.200
Seventeen parcels make four full bundles and

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:08.130
one loose parcel.

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:11.090
How many more parcels would fill that last bundle?

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:11.990
Three.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.510
The remainder tells us what is already there.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:16.430
The gap tells us what is missing.

00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:20.720
A parcel label earns a square stamp if its number

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.290
divides evenly by four.

00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:25.100
It earns a triangle stamp if the number divides

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.310
evenly by three.

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.490
The four labels are three hundred and twenty four,

00:00:29.800 --> 00:00:31.190
three hundred and forty four,

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:32.890
three hundred and fifty four,

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.730
and three hundred and eighty four.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.130
Which get both stamps?

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:42.610
For the square stamp, inspect the last two digits.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.040
A hundred is divisible by four, so the hundreds place

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.470
cannot spoil the test.

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:51.070
Twenty four, forty four and eighty four pass.

00:00:51.920 --> 00:00:52.930
Fifty four fails.

00:00:53.240 --> 00:00:55.150
For the triangle stamp, add the digits.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:58.850
The three survivors have sums nine, eleven and fifteen.

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:02.030
Only the first and last labels get both stamps.

00:01:03.110 --> 00:01:06.050
A crate contains between twenty three and fifty nine

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:07.980
parcels, including both ends.

00:01:08.590 --> 00:01:09.340
Add one parcel.

00:01:10.010 --> 00:01:13.050
Now the crate can be packed exactly in fours, and it can

00:01:13.050 --> 00:01:15.300
also be packed exactly in sixes.

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:18.140
How many different original loads could it have held?

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:24.290
Push the whole interval one place to the right.

00:01:24.720 --> 00:01:26.630
Twenty three becomes twenty four,

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:28.430
and fifty nine becomes sixty.

00:01:29.260 --> 00:01:32.100
We now need numbers in this shifted interval that the

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:34.870
groups of four and the groups of six both reach.

00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:38.200
Mark the multiples of four above the line,

00:01:38.200 --> 00:01:40.150
and the multiples of six below.

00:01:40.700 --> 00:01:42.830
Both first meet at twelve after zero.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:45.350
The same arrangement repeats every twelve.

00:01:46.100 --> 00:01:48.470
In our shifted interval, the shared stops are

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:51.710
twenty four, thirty six, forty eight and sixty.

00:01:52.840 --> 00:01:55.340
Each shared stop describes the crate after

00:01:55.340 --> 00:01:56.210
adding a parcel.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:57.930
Take one away from each.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:01.070
The original loads are twenty three, thirty five,

00:02:01.220 --> 00:02:02.790
forty seven and fifty nine.

00:02:03.440 --> 00:02:04.050
Four possibilities.

00:02:05.020 --> 00:02:05.830
Check the last one.

00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.440
The extra parcel makes sixty, which packs as fifteen

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.520
fours or ten sixes.

00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:15.250
A tempting answer keeps sixty and loses twenty three.

00:02:15.820 --> 00:02:17.550
That mixes two different number lines.

00:02:18.140 --> 00:02:20.230
Move the end stops when you change the quantity.

00:02:21.060 --> 00:02:23.770
Test the new quantity, then undo the change.

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:27.500
For a different remainder problem, first ask whether one

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:29.910
small move can make both divisions exact.
